            </article>

            <!-- Squeak peek bottom sheet -->
            <m3e-bottom-sheet p-ref="tipsSheet" modal>
                <h2 class="subsection-title">Squeak peek: cooking tips</h2>
                <m3e-list>
                    <m3e-list-item>Make sure the Camembert is properly ripe. It should give slightly when pressed in the centre. An under-ripe Camembert won't melt to that gorgeous gooey consistency.</m3e-list-item>
                    <m3e-list-item>Keep it in the wooden box for baking. The box acts as a natural vessel and stops the cheese from spreading out into a flat puddle.</m3e-list-item>
                    <m3e-list-item>Serve immediately, because it sets as it cools. If it does firm up, a quick blast back in the oven for 5 minutes will bring it back to life.</m3e-list-item>
                </m3e-list>
            </m3e-bottom-sheet>

            <!-- Cheese fact dialog -->
            <m3e-dialog p-ref="cheeseFact" headline="Cheese fact!">
                <p class="prose">Camembert was first made in Normandy, France, in 1791 by Marie Harel. Legend says she learned the technique from a priest she sheltered during the Revolution.</p>
                <m3e-button variant="filled" slot="actions" p-on:click="closeCheeseFact">Gouda to know!</m3e-button>
            </m3e-dialog>

